data from the office for national  statistics shows another big rise in  covid cases more than four and a quarter  million people in the uk had the disease  in the week to last saturday up a  million in just seven days look at a  chart and you can see how the infections  have surged since the second half of  february driven by the more infectious  ba2 subvariant of omicron and the  lifting of all restrictions and now  close to the record peak for the  pandemic in january the highest rates  are in young children more than eight  percent had coveted but there will be  more concern that five percent of people  over 70 had the virus and they're of  course much more likely to become  seriously ill so are they well data from  the uk health security agency shows  hospital admissions and people over the  age of 75 are now higher than they were  at the peak of the omicron wave at the  start of the year the hospitalization  rate for every 100 000 people over 85  was 178 last week compared to 158 in  january and in people between 75 and 84  it was 74 last week compared to 70 in  the january peak now that looks alarming  but the health secretary has said 60  percent of people testing positive in  hospital are being admitted to treat  other conditions not covered  for now the vaccine appears to be  holding up reasonably well but it's  beginning to wane as shown by other data  from the health security agency  it looked at people with respiratory  disease needing oxygen ventilation or  critical care now immediately after the  booster the jab was 95 effective at  preventing such serious disease but 15  weeks after the booster that had dropped  to 87  it's a relatively small drop but if lots  of older people are being infected it  could add up to lots more hospital  admissions  and that's why everyone over 75 is now  being urged to book a spring booster to  top up their immunity
 

